WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.960
Three out of four Indian women are unemployed.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:12.240
That’s more than Saudi Arabia — a country where&nbsp;
women have only been allowed to drive since 2018.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:19.800
This is no statistical sleight of hand. In fact,&nbsp;
“three out of four” is wildly conservative.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.040
This number — called the “labor force&nbsp;
participation rate” includes both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:29.960
anyone with or without a job — as long&nbsp;
as they’re looking, however passively.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:38.080
Of those 24% of women technically in the&nbsp;
“labor force”, many don’t actually work.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.320
Then there’s the question of&nbsp;
“what counts as ‘employment’?”

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:48.000
In this case, the answer is as little&nbsp;
as one hour each week of paid labor.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.880
Put differently: of India’s 1.4&nbsp;
billion people, 681 million are women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:03.160
509 million of those are at least 15 years old,&nbsp;
122 million officially in the “labor force”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:11.520
and a tiny 38 million — 8.8% — according to&nbsp;
one Indian think-tank — meaningfully employed.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.000
In a country of 1.4 billion, in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.680
just 38 million women have jobs.&nbsp;
The United States, by contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:27.360
has twice as many women employed — in absolute&nbsp;
terms — with only one-fourth the population.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.840
How can that possibly be true?

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.160
Could it be that Indian households are now rich&nbsp;
enough to comfortably subsist on one income?

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:43.840
No, far from it. The country’s per&nbsp;
capita GDP is less than $2,400 — just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.360
one-fifth the world average and where&nbsp;
China was about twenty years ago.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.680
Is it because women lack access to education?

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.080
According to the World Bank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.360
“better educated women are more likely to&nbsp;
participate in the formal labor market”.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.280
Again, no.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.040
Strangely, in India, educated women&nbsp;
are less likely to find employment.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.280
College enrollment has soared since the turn&nbsp;
of the century, yet women’s participation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:17.960
the labor force has actually been on&nbsp;
a steady decline until very recently.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:24.560
Perhaps it’s precisely because women are&nbsp;
in school — studying, rather than working.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.760
Nope. It’s not that either, since older&nbsp;
women are also missing from the labor force.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.800
Finally, could it be an issue of&nbsp;
personal safety or cultural expectations?

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.520
Certainly this is a major piece of the puzzle.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.720
India is one of the world’s most&nbsp;
dangerous countries for women.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.680
Just getting to and from work safely&nbsp;
presents a not insignificant challenge.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:58.240
Meanwhile, 99% of Indian women eventually get&nbsp;
married, 90% of those marriages are arranged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.040
and women are expected to do&nbsp;
nearly all of the housework.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.280
An extraordinary six percent of men report doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:11.200
any cooking whatsoever and only&nbsp;
8% do at least some cleaning.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.400
More of women’s time spent cooking and&nbsp;
cleaning is less time for paid labor.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.080
Case: closed.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.160
…Except, hang on — take a look at India’s&nbsp;
neighbor-on-three-sides, Bangladesh.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.200
The two countries rank nearly identically on the&nbsp;
“Women’s Peace and Security Index” — that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:38.920
very poorly. They share many of the same&nbsp;
cultural norms, practices, and beliefs.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.760
…And yet, women in Bangladesh&nbsp;
work at a much higher rate.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:49.960
Clearly there’s much more to this&nbsp;
story. What’s really going on?

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:53.200
The answer lies in this graph.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.080
But first, a brief aside.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.000
In India, like many countries,&nbsp;
a family’s honor depends on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.160
“purity” of its daughters. Any time&nbsp;
spent outside the home — for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:11.880
at work — exposes women to accusations of&nbsp;
promiscuity, tarnishing her family’s honor.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.680
Parents, therefore, strongly pressure&nbsp;
their daughters to stay home.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:23.040
Of course, working also has its benefits —&nbsp;
children are expected to contribute financially.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:30.080
This presents a trade-off between income and&nbsp;
honor. And since honor is more-or-less a constant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.920
the important variable here are&nbsp;
wages — for any given family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.880
is the extra money generated&nbsp;
by allowing your daughter to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:44.480
work worth the damage to your reputation,&nbsp;
and, by extension, her marriage prospects?

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.720
When families are poor, this loss&nbsp;
of honor is strongly preferable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.560
the alternative — starvation. Women&nbsp;
have no choice; they have to work.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.880
Conversely, when wages are high,&nbsp;
they offset this honor “penalty”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.200
causing women to rejoin the workforce.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.720
India, as a country, is stuck at&nbsp;
the bottom of this “U” curve — what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.200
Dr. Alice Evans of King's College&nbsp;
London calls the “patrilineal trap”.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:17.360
Wages are just barely high enough that&nbsp;
families can scrape by on one income,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.440
but not high enough to draw&nbsp;
women back to the workplace.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.840
Women are trapped between low&nbsp;
wages and suffocating social norms.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:30.880
They do go to college — though&nbsp;
mainly to find a husband.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.240
And they do want to work. In a recent survey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.520
75% of teenage girls said they hoped&nbsp;
to find a job after graduating.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.480
The problem is that wages aren’t high&nbsp;
enough to convince their families.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:51.120
The real mystery, then, is why Indian&nbsp;
wages are still so incredibly low.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.480
Since 1990, its economy has grown at an impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:02.040
average rate of 6% a year — not so&nbsp;
far behind China’s miraculous 8.8%.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:08.560
Back then, India was only the world’s&nbsp;
twelfth-largest economy. Today, it’s the fifth.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.600
Yet somehow this spectacular&nbsp;
growth failed to create any jobs.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.880
Now, economists call the number of new jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:21.160
produced for every 1% change in&nbsp;
GDP the “elasticity of labor”.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:28.960
Well, between 2011 and 19, the Indian labor&nbsp;
force grew by 0.26%, imputing an elasticity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:37.320
of 0.04 — which is a polite way of saying&nbsp;
virtually zero jobs were created over 10 years.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:44.360
This implies that if its economy were to double&nbsp;
overnight, employment would only increase by 4%.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:54.120
Between 1999 and 2005, 60 million new jobs&nbsp;
appeared. Between 2004 and 10, just 3.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.160
The result is fierce competition&nbsp;
for every last scrap of paid work.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:04.600
In 2019, 12 million people&nbsp;
applied for 35,000 railroad jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:09.720
and 1.7 million for 1,500&nbsp;
jobs at a state-owned bank.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.120
Most of these applicants are&nbsp;
young, and the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:20.960
college-educated. Those who are both young and&nbsp;
college-educated don’t stand a chance. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:27.320
42% of Indians under 25 with&nbsp;
graduate degrees can’t find work.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:33.360
So, what do all these young scholars do?&nbsp;
They go back to school, of course — fueling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.520
a race to acquire an increasing number of&nbsp;
unnecessary credentials or else fall behind.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:47.400
Others simply lose hope and spend their days doing&nbsp;
what Indians call “timepass” — aimless leisure.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.480
Now, one clue to the whereabouts of&nbsp;
these missing jobs is the country’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:59.760
most common occupation — not salesperson,&nbsp;
waiter, or janitor but “CEO”. Truly.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:06.000
You see, contrary to the image of sweatshops many&nbsp;
around the world imagine, Indian employers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.360
actually quite tightly regulated. Legally, they&nbsp;
must provide workers with paid leave, pensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:18.640
and health insurance. They even need permission&nbsp;
from the government to fire or lay them off.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.120
…At least, in principle.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:27.360
In practice, most of these laws only apply&nbsp;
to businesses with 10 or more employees.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.600
And, unrelatedly, for some odd reason Indian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:35.680
companies seem to have decided nine&nbsp;
is the optimal number of workers.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.920
So, what do you know — the vast majority of&nbsp;
businesses are exempt from these regulations.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:45.760
Another reason they stay&nbsp;
small is to evade corruption.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.920
Companies with 10 or more workers&nbsp;
are visited by labor inspectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.440
many of which enforce arcane&nbsp;
rules to extract bribes.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:59.760
Thus, whereas China has a healthy balance of&nbsp;
mid-size companies — many of which have between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:07.880
51 and 200 workers, over 80% of Indian apparel&nbsp;
firms, for instance, have less than eight.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.000
With so many small businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.680
it’s no wonder India has perhaps the&nbsp;
world’s highest density of “CEOs”.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.960
As companies grow, rather than hire more workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.760
they simply outsource this additional&nbsp;
labor to other small companies.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:31.360
But this loophole — clever as it is — has a&nbsp;
cost: smaller companies are less productive.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:39.040
On average, those with 50-199 workers are about&nbsp;
60% less productive than those with over 200,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:44.280
and those with 5-49 workers&nbsp;
about 90% less productive.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:48.680
Lower productivity means lower wages. Lower wages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:54.200
mean less consumer demand. And&nbsp;
less demand means fewer jobs.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:58.000
…Not just fewer but also lower quality.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:06.840
Only 21% of Indian workers earn regular wages.&nbsp;
Of them, 46% don’t receive any paid leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:14.560
53% don’t receive social security, and&nbsp;
nearly 60% have no written contract at all.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.440
This “flexibility” — as employers call it — is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.280
stressful and unpredictable&nbsp;
during the best of times.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.320
When things are anything less than&nbsp;
peachy, these informal jobs become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:30.960
downright catastrophic. Like,&nbsp;
say, during a global pandemic.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:35.200
In 2020, the Prime Minister declared&nbsp;
what began as a three-week national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.240
lockdown which immediately put&nbsp;
100 million Indians out of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:44.280
who fled en masse to the countryside&nbsp;
— the only place they could go.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:47.640
By December of that year, 75 million people had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:53.760
been plunged below the poverty line of&nbsp;
$2 a day, according to Pew Research.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:59.040
But there’s another, bigger, structural,&nbsp;
reason why India has so few jobs.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.440
Of the countries that successfully&nbsp;
escaped poverty in the last hundred years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:09.120
almost all have pursued the same,&nbsp;
tried and true development recipe:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:15.760
a rapid progression from agriculture to&nbsp;
manufacturing to services — in that order.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:22.120
It worked for Taiwan, Hong Kong,&nbsp;
Singapore, South Korea, and now China.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:26.960
India, on the other hand, seems&nbsp;
to have somehow missed this memo.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.800
By the time its economy&nbsp;
liberalized in the early 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:34.280
China was already ten years into this journey.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:38.160
And having started so late, Indian&nbsp;
factories couldn’t compete with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.840
Chinese competitors. So, they largely gave up.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:47.760
Instead, India skipped straight from&nbsp;
agriculture to services — leaving out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:52.560
that very critical middle step&nbsp;
in the recipe: manufacturing.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:57.200
Today, Vietnam — with a population of&nbsp;
less than a hundred million — exports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.960
more clothing than India, with&nbsp;
a population of 1.4 billion.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:09.040
Now, there’s nothing wrong with services. Nearly&nbsp;
all rich countries are service economies — from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:15.760
the U.S. to Japan and Switzerland. So&nbsp;
you might think India found a shortcut.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:22.560
The country has an impressive depth of finance,&nbsp;
insurance, real estate, and technology expertise.

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:28.960
Deloitte, Goldman, and Tata pay their Indian&nbsp;
consultants and bankers top-dollar — much more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.920
of course, than most factory workers.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:36.440
The problem is that there just&nbsp;
aren’t that many of these jobs.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:39.200
As Indian farmers have grown more productive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:45.880
as they have across the globe, agriculture has&nbsp;
been on a steady decline. No surprise there.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:50.560
Usually, however, all these farmers&nbsp;
would become factory workers. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.440
labor-intensive industry replaced with another.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:58.840
But, as you can see, manufacturing&nbsp;
hasn’t absorbed these displaced farmers.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.720
Sure, these new service jobs have&nbsp;
helped — just not nearly enough.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:11.160
So, in order to make up the difference,&nbsp;
Indians have flocked to construction.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:16.160
These jobs are often temporary,&nbsp;
low-paid, and physically dangerous.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:19.920
But this trend is particularly bad for women.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:25.600
In East Asia, where manufacturing replaced&nbsp;
agriculture — women became factory workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:30.840
which slowly influenced social expectations&nbsp;
and afforded them newfound freedom.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:34.520
In India, women were pushed out&nbsp;
of farming but discouraged from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:40.480
taking on construction. A few lucky&nbsp;
women secured lucrative service jobs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:47.640
But the vast majority simply left the labor&nbsp;
market altogether. They had nowhere to go.

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:52.560
This is why India’s economy continued&nbsp;
to grow — those new service jobs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:58.760
very very productive — while the number of&nbsp;
jobs did not — they’re few and far between.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:04.800
It’s why women in Bangladesh work at much&nbsp;
higher rates — its many factories provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:11.320
them with a socially-acceptable place to&nbsp;
do so, whereas India has no such scale.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:16.960
What India does have — that&nbsp;
most of Asia does not — is time.

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:21.400
China’s population peaked last year&nbsp;
and its labor force is expected to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:26.240
decrease by about 1% every year starting in 2030.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:30.760
India, meanwhile, is still&nbsp;
very much young and growing.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.560
40% of its population is under the age of 25,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:44.720
and 53% under 30. Its median age is a youthful&nbsp;
28, compared to China’s 38, or Japan’s 48.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:48.040
With any luck, India has about&nbsp;
two-decades remaining in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:51.960
“demographic window” — a critical&nbsp;
transition period during which a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:57.480
country has a large number of working-age&nbsp;
people compared to children and retirees.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.720
If the country can harness this labor,&nbsp;
it could still experience a taste of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:07.320
demographic dividend that propelled China&nbsp;
from a poor to a middle-income country.

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.560
By merely employing women at the same rate as men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:16.640
according to Bloomberg, India’s economy&nbsp;
could grow by nearly a third by 2050.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:23.400
But, if it wastes this opportunity, these excess&nbsp;
young people could become a demographic burden.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:27.120
In that case, it would be stuck&nbsp;
with millions of angry, aimless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:31.880
and unemployed 20, 30, and 40 year-olds.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:36.760
Simply put: India has no time to&nbsp;
waste, which means it may need some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:42.520
help from David Allen in his book Getting&nbsp;
Things Done on today’s sponsor, Imprint.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:46.560
In it, for example, I learned the 5&nbsp;
stages of being productive and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:50.360
